WEBVTT

00:00:00.017 --> 00:00:04.457
We will move from concepts to implementation gradually.

00:00:04.457 --> 00:00:06.792
First, we build the relational mental model.

00:00:06.792 --> 00:00:13.087
You will identify entities, attributes, row grain, keys, and relationships.

00:00:13.087 --> 00:00:15.447
Then we focus on protection.

00:00:15.447 --> 00:00:23.472
Constraints and normalization help keep the data consistent even when many users or applications interact with

00:00:23.472 --> 00:00:27.282
the database. After that, we connect the design to PostgreSQL.

00:00:27.282 --> 00:00:33.012
You will run completed examples before you are asked to change or write small pieces yourself.

00:00:33.012 --> 00:00:38.987
The coding progression is intentional: run, change, complete, and then write.

00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:43.032
You are not expected to memorize a long CREATE TABLE script.

00:00:43.032 --> 00:00:48.302
Instead, learn how to read the pieces and explain why each one exists.

00:00:48.302 --> 00:00:54.792
Connect this idea to a university system separating students, courses, sections, and enrollments.

00:00:54.792 --> 00:00:59.917
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:09.302
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:14.557
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:14.557 --> 00:01:20.109
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
